{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>And HaShem set a sign for Cain, that none who found him should strike him.</b> Our Sages differed as to the nature of this sign — what it was. In the Midrash (Genesis Rabbah 22:27) they said: the orb of the sun shone upon him, etc. And it is difficult: how would the shining of the sun suffice to ensure that none who found him should strike him? For according to the line of justice, 'the wicked are likened to darkness' — so how could the sun shine upon him? And the explanation of the matter appears to be this, because it is stated (Exodus 22:1): 'If the thief be found breaking in, and he is struck and dies, there is no blood-guilt; if the sun has risen upon him, there is blood-guilt for him.' So too Cain was a thief, for he would steal the mind of the Most High, as Rashi explained in this passage (Genesis 4:9), and he was going about as one in a breach, for HaShem hid His face from him and he was banished from the Holy Land — the land upon which the eyes of HaShem always look — and He banished him outside the Land, where one who dwells there is like one who has no god, on account of the hiding of His face, blessed be He, as it is stated: 'and from Your face I will hide.' And then he was like one found in a breach, for he was fleeing from place to place, and every fugitive hides himself in secret. And if so, it is obvious that anyone who found him would kill him, for there is no blood-guilt for him, since he was found in a breach. Therefore HaShem caused the sun to shine upon him in all the places where he went, to indicate that the law of one found in a breach does not apply to him, but rather the law of one upon whom the sun has risen — for whom there is blood-guilt, and whoever kills him is executed for it — and this is a decisive sign that none who found him should strike him. And you will know and understand from the expression 'all who find me' — why did it suspend the matter upon 'finding him'? It is obvious that they would not kill someone whom they do not find! He should have said, 'every creature will kill me!' Rather, this expression teaches that he feared lest he be subject to the law of one found in a breach, for with that, the language of 'finding' applies, as it is stated: 'If the thief be found in the breach.' And through the shining of the sun, none who found him would strike him in that breach in which he hid himself on account of his flight from place to place. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:03.228758586Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}